Related to #383 — same function, different failure mode: that one over-populates `prefetch`, this one silently under-populates `styles`.

## Environment

- `vue-bundle-renderer` 2.3.1 **and** 2.3.2 (verified against both dist builds)
- Node 22

## Summary

`precomputeDependencies()` can return a dependency set where a chunk's `styles` does not contain the styles of one of its own direct `imports`. Which chunks are affected depends on the iteration order of `Object.keys(manifest)`, so two builds with an identical module graph can produce different — and incorrect — results.

## Minimal repro, no Nuxt involved

```js
// npm i vue-bundle-renderer@2.3.2
import { precomputeDependencies } from 'vue-bundle-renderer'

const build = (order) => {
  const m = {}
  for (const k of order) {
    if (k === 'a.js')       m[k] = { file: 'a.js', resourceType: 'script', imports: ['_shared.js'] }
    if (k === '_shared.js') m[k] = { file: 'shared.js', resourceType: 'script', css: ['shared.css'], imports: ['b.js'] }
    if (k === 'b.js')       m[k] = { file: 'b.js', resourceType: 'script', imports: ['a.js'] }
    if (k === 'shared.css') m[k] = { file: 'shared.css', resourceType: 'style' }
  }
  return m
}

for (const order of [
  ['a.js', '_shared.js', 'b.js', 'shared.css'],
  ['b.js', 'a.js', '_shared.js', 'shared.css'],
]) {
  const out = precomputeDependencies(build(order))
  console.log('key order:', order.join(', '))
  for (const id of ['a.js', '_shared.js', 'b.js']) {
    console.log('  ', id, JSON.stringify(Object.keys(out.dependencies[id].styles)))
  }
}
```

The graph is `a.js --static--> _shared.js --static--> b.js --static--> a.js`, and `shared.css` is owned by `_shared.js`. Every chunk transitively reaches `shared.css`. Only the key order differs between the two runs — no `dynamicImports` are involved at all.

### Actual

```
key order: a.js, _shared.js, b.js, shared.css
   a.js       ["shared.css"]
   _shared.js ["shared.css"]
   b.js       []              <-- shared.css lost
key order: b.js, a.js, _shared.js, shared.css
   a.js       ["shared.css"]
   _shared.js ["shared.css"]
   b.js       ["shared.css"]
```

### Expected

`b.js` resolves to `["shared.css"]` in both cases. More generally, the invariant `styles(P) ⊇ styles(c)` should hold for every `c ∈ P.imports`.

## Where it comes from, as far as I can tell

In `computeDependencies`:

```js
if (dependencies[id]) return dependencies[id]
if (computing.has(id)) return { scripts: {}, styles: {}, preload: {}, prefetch: {} }  // cycle guard
// ...
for (const depId of meta.imports || []) {
  const depDeps = computeDependencies(depId)
  Object.assign(deps.styles, depDeps.styles)
  // ...
}
// ...
dependencies[id] = deps   // memoised, including the truncated result
```

When a cycle is entered, the guard returns an empty set. That is fine for the re-entrant call itself, but the **caller** merges that empty set and its own — now incomplete — result is memoised permanently. Because the driver is

```js
for (const moduleId of Object.keys(manifest)) computeDependencies(moduleId)
```

whichever node the traversal enters the cycle from first determines which chunks end up truncated.

## Real-world impact

Found while debugging a Nuxt 4.5.1 app with `features.inlineStyles: false`. In that mode the SSR renderer emits `<link rel="stylesheet">` from `getRequestDependencies(...).styles`, so a truncated entry means a rendered component's CSS never reaches the initial HTML — the page renders unstyled until client JS loads (FOUC / CLS).

A Nuxt chunk graph is cyclic by construction: `layouts/default.vue --imports--> entry.js --dynamicImports--> layouts/default.vue`.

Changing a single Vite `define` — which alters module contents but not the shape of the graph — reordered chunk names and changed the number of inconsistent `parent ⊉ child` edges from **17** to **119** out of 2116, silently dropping the CSS of different components between otherwise identical builds. Concretely, `button.css` disappeared from the layout's `styles`, so every button on the site rendered unstyled without JS.

Note on 2.3.2: removing the `dynamicImports` recursion (the change discussed in #383) happens to break that particular Nuxt cycle, which masks this bug in a Nuxt app. The cycle guard itself is unchanged, and the repro above still fails on 2.3.2 because it uses a purely static cycle.

## Suggested fix

Handle cycles instead of memoising a truncated result — e.g.:

- iterate to a fixed point, or
- compute strongly connected components and give all members of an SCC the same merged dependency set, or
- at minimum, do not cache a result that was computed while the cycle guard fired.

As a workaround, consumers can flatten the transitive CSS closure over static `imports` into each chunk's `css` before calling `precomputeDependencies`, which makes the result order-independent.
